  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n\nA round of thunderstorms has led to rain cooled air dropping heat\nbelow advisory levels this evening. Later tonight, additional storm\ndevelopment is possible after midnight with storms possibly dropping\nsouth during the second half of the overnight.\n\nIf a large thunderstorm complex fails to form and move through the\narea overnight, low-level jet driven thunderstorms may form \nthroughout the area into Saturday morning ahead of an approaching \nshortwave. This is typically found on the western edge of the \nthunderstorm activity from the previous day. As temperatures warm up \nSaturday morning, additional thunderstorms would then develop with \nthe renewed energy. Right now, the confidence in this occurring is \nover southwest portions of the area (southeast Indiana, southwest \nOhio, northern Kentucky). A damaging wind threat would once again \nexist with these thunderstorms. \n\nOverall, confidence remains low for any particular scenario. The\nridge breakdown will occur into the weekend, but the ultimate impacts\nacross the area are uncertain. \n\nWith PWATs well above normal, multiple thunderstorm complexes will\nprovide the opportunity for locally heavy rainfall and flash\nflooding, especially for areas where storms move over repeatedly.",
